Or make wishing for something you haven't seen yet much less likely to work
(say, 25-50% instead of 100%).  That way, you can replace your bag of
holding if you blow it up, and if you happen to get an early wish (and thus
haven't seen anything exciting), you could try to get something really
useful.  Heck, make the probability inversely proportional to the number of
"known" items, so if you've only identified (or named), say, 25 items,
there's still a very high percentage you'll get what you want, but by the
time you've made it to the castle (and have ID'd most of the potions,
scrolls, etc.), it's _very_ hard to wish up a full AK, unless you're
replacing stuff you already had.  (Of course, this could also lead to people
intentionally _not_ genociding [master] mind flayers, in order to forget
items, which would be interesting; it also makes scrolls of amnesia fairly
powerful, at least potentially...I'm not sure if it'd be an abuse, or if the
downsides would automatically ballance it...)

Precedents: wishing for Artifacts is already inversely proportional to the
seen artifact count, and generally you have a small but non-zero chance of
writing a spellbook/scroll you haven't actually seen yet.  So this YANI
isn't as far out there as it may seem...

